On 08/03/2010 07:20 PM, Pierre Abbat wrote:
> I've used git, but am pretty new to it. What's github like?
>
> Pierre
>

Github offers free hosting for open source projects and paid for hosting
if you want non-public repositories. They have a nice web interface for
all their stuff. Here's a few nice things to look at:

A project overview page with the last commit message, the files and a
readme file.

http://github.com/lojban/lojbanquest


A list of all commits.

http://github.com/lojban/lojbanquest/commits/master


A commit view with a big diff.

http://github.com/lojban/lojbanquest/commit/7e88e634c6a1b342a952f86cccb8d8d806d4758c


The "network graph visualizer" that shows all commits on all forks.

http://github.com/blog/39-say-hello-to-the-network-graph-visualizer


You can mark other repositories or accounts as "watched" and you will
get their activities in your private news feed. There's a git-based
nopaste-like service called "gist", every projects gets an issue tracker
and a wiki, as well as a website they can (but not have to) use.

The web interface enables you to easily fork projects and once you've
made changes, you can send a pull-request to the repository you forked
from with one click. There's a view to compare branches and different
repositories. You can edit files directly in the web interface.


In short, github makes git hosting easy and beautiful.

